Absolutely. Interfaces are one of the most asked Java interview topics, especially in Spring Boot because Spring heavily relies on interfaces (JpaRepository, CrudRepository, List, Map, Runnable, Comparable, etc.).

Below is a complete interview guide from basic → advanced → FAANG-level.

LEVEL 1 (Basic)
1. What is an Interface?
Answer

An interface is a blueprint (contract) that defines what a class should do, not how it should do it.

Example

interface Animal {
    void sound();
}

class Dog implements Animal {

    @Override
    public void sound() {
        System.out.println("Bark");
    }
}
2. Why do we use Interfaces?
Achieve abstraction
Multiple inheritance
Loose coupling
Runtime polymorphism
3. Can we create an object of an Interface?

No.

Animal a = new Animal(); // ❌
4. How do we create an object?

Using implementing class.

Animal a = new Dog();
5. Why?

Interface has no complete implementation.

6. Can Interface have Constructors?

❌ No

Reason:

Constructor initializes an object.

Interfaces cannot have objects.

7. Can Interface have Variables?

Yes.

interface Demo{

    int x = 10;
}

Actually Java converts it into

public static final int x = 10;

All variables are

public
static
final

automatically.

8. Can we modify Interface variables?
Demo.x = 20;

❌ No

Because variables are final.

9. Can Interface have Instance Variables?

No.

int age;

❌ Illegal

Because every variable becomes static final.

10. Can Interface have Static Variables?

Yes.

static int x = 10;

Actually already static.

LEVEL 2
11. Can Interface have Methods?

Yes.

Before Java 8

Only abstract methods.

After Java 8

abstract
default
static

After Java 9

private
private static
12. Default Methods
interface Animal{

    default void eat(){

        System.out.println("Eating");
    }
}

Purpose

Backward compatibility.

13. Static Methods
interface Animal{

    static void show(){

    }
}

Call

Animal.show();
14. Private Methods

Java 9

interface Animal{

    private void helper(){

    }
}

Used only inside interface.

15. Can Interface have Abstract Methods?

Yes.

void show();

Compiler makes it

public abstract void show();
16. Can Abstract Method be Final?

No.

Contradiction.

17. Can Abstract Method be Static?

No.

18. Can Abstract Method be Private?

No.

Private methods cannot be overridden.

19. Can Interface have Final Methods?

No.

All abstract methods must be overridden.

