❌

24. Can Class implement multiple Interfaces?

Yes.

class Demo implements A,B,C{

}
25. Why?

Multiple inheritance of type.

No ambiguity because interfaces originally had no implementation.

LEVEL 4
26. Difference between Abstract Class and Interface?
Abstract Class	Interface
Constructor	Yes
Instance Variable	Yes
Multiple Inheritance	No
Multiple Interface	Yes
State	Yes
100% abstraction	No
27. Why are Interface methods public?

Because implementing classes can be anywhere.

They must be accessible.

28. Why Interface variables are public static final?

Imagine

interface Demo{

    int x=10;
}

If variables weren't static,

every object would need separate copy.

But interface has no objects.

29. Can Interface have Main Method?

YES

interface Demo{

    public static void main(String args[]){

        System.out.println("Hello");
    }
}

Runs perfectly.

30. Can Interface be Final?

No.

Need inheritance.

31. Can Interface be Abstract?

Actually

Every interface is abstract.

Compiler internally writes

abstract interface Demo{

}
LEVEL 5
32. Diamond Problem
interface A{

    default void show(){

    }
}

interface B{

    default void show(){

    }
}

class C implements A,B{

}

Compile Error.

Must override.

@Override
public void show(){

    A.super.show();
}
33. Why?

Compiler doesn't know

A.show()

or

B.show()
34. Functional Interface

Contains exactly one abstract method.

@FunctionalInterface
interface Demo{

    void show();
}

Used by

Lambda
Streams
35. Marker Interface

No methods.

Example

Serializable

Cloneable

RandomAccess

Purpose

Provide metadata.

36. SAM Interface

Single Abstract Method Interface.

Same as Functional Interface.

37. Can Functional Interface have Default Methods?

Yes.

@FunctionalInterface
interface Demo{

    void show();

    default void helper(){

    }
}

Still Functional.

38. Can Functional Interface have Static Methods?

Yes.

39. Can Functional Interface have Private Methods?

Yes.

40. Can Functional Interface extend another Interface?

Yes.

Only if total abstract methods remain one.

LEVEL 6
41. Why is Spring using Interfaces everywhere?

Example

@Service
class EmailService implements Notification{

}

Later

@Service
class SmsService implements Notification{

}

Application code

Notification notification;

Can switch implementation without changing client code.

Loose coupling.

42. Why does Spring recommend programming to Interface?

Instead of

EmailService service=new EmailService();

Use

Notification service;

Tomorrow

SmsService

or

PushNotification

No code changes.

43. Why does JpaRepository use Interface?
public interface UserRepository
extends JpaRepository<User,Integer>{

}

Spring creates implementation at runtime.

No need to write CRUD methods.

44. Can Interface contain Object methods?
equals()

hashCode()

toString()

Yes.

45. Can Interface Override Object methods?

Implementing class does.

46. Why can't Interface have Protected Methods?

Because interface methods are meant to define a public contract. protected would restrict access and defeat that purpose.

47. Can Interface have Nested Interface?

Yes.

interface A{

    interface B{

    }
}
48. Can Interface have Nested Class?

Yes.

interface A{

    class B{

    }
}

It is implicitly public static.

49. Can Anonymous Class implement Interface?

Yes.

Runnable r = new Runnable() {

    @Override
    public void run() {
        System.out.println("Running");
    }
};
50. Can Lambda implement Interface?

Only Functional Interface.

Runnable r = () -> System.out.println("Running");
51. Interface vs Composition

Don't confuse them.

Interface → What behavior an object promises.
Composition → How an object is built using other objects.

Example:

interface Engine {
    void start();
}

class PetrolEngine implements Engine {
    public void start() {
        System.out.println("Petrol Engine");
    }
}

class Car {

    private Engine engine; // Composition + programming to an interface

    Car(Engine engine) {
        this.engine = engine;
    }
}

Here:

PetrolEngine implements Engine → Interface (contract)
Car has an Engine → Composition/aggregation depending on ownership
